  "worked_solution": "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{\u2032}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}+{y}^{2}+2y,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)={y}^{3}+{y}^{2}+2y.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=0$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(0)={0}^{3}+{0}^{2}+2(0)=0.$$\\,\u2713$\n\n- $y=1$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(1)={1}^{3}+{1}^{2}+2(1)=4\\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=2$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(2)={2}^{3}+{2}^{2}+2(2)=16\\ne 0.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
